      "statement": "The narrative of Esther is historical in substance.",
      "layer": "reconstruction",
      "passage": "Esth.2.17",
      "classification": "disputed",
      "reasoning": "Keil defends the book's historical character against Bleek and Bertheau (as he reports them), citing its accurate Persian names, customs and court detail and the reference to the royal chronicles. Bertheau (via Keil) grants the author's knowledge of Persian affairs but not of the course of events. Herodotus (read directly) names Xerxes's wife Amestris (7.61; 9.108–113) and nowhere mentions Vashti or Esther; no source read reconciles these. Guzik's statement (after Baldwin) that Herodotus mentions Hegai is not borne out: Herodotus's Hegias (9.33) is the brother of an Elean seer.",
